[quote="twoeyedjack"]

I went to my pictures of the character book that I took at the demo, and here are all the references to defensive/healing type mechanics that I could see:

Defend

Your hero is always taking steps to protect themselves, but there are times when they want to focus on protecting themselves or someone else. In a Defend action, you describe what you hero is doing to avoid or reduce an Attack. Assemble your dice pool, roll those dice, and note your result. When you or the target you are Defending would next lose Health before your next turn, reduce the amount taken by your Defend result. If you or the target you&#39;re Defending are not Attacked before your next turn, the effect is wasted.If you are under the effect of multiple Defend actions, choose the higher result to use; do not combine them.

&nbsp;

There is also the fact that as you move from one zone to the next from taking damage, more abilities get unlocked and are available for use. So while you might consider taking damage again and again to be ridiculous, it does result in your character becoming more powerful and having more options to fight back or react to incoming damage. And even when you are Out, you still provide a unique ability for anyone on your team to use, just like in the card game.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

I&#39;ll try to respond in stages.

&nbsp;

That first line of the Defend action bothers me. &quot;Your hero is always taking steps to protect themselves&quot; Yet, mechanically, that is not the case at all. There is no way to dodge or mitigate anything that is &quot;always&quot; on unless you have armor. I think this is what leads me to my initialy confusion. I was being told that I was also defending myself, while doing my best impression of a punching bag that even the weakest foes and minions could easily damage. I come from the traditions of DnD and other systems, where attacks can miss due to armor, this is more a culture shock than anything else and that phrasing does not help explain to new players how the system works in mechanical reality.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The next part is perhaps due to the combat I am remembering. AZ and Legacy were alone, and we had about 6-7 robots attacking us. At three bots each, and all of our attack actions only able to target a single bot until we lost enough health, we were looking at approximately three rounds of combat. Using just average numbers from the d6&#39;s, I was looking at 10 damage, followed by 7 and then 3. 20 damage of my 29 starting health, dropping me into red from the very first scene of the entire game.

&nbsp;

That does make me more powerful, but it also puts me closing to being dropped to incap, which is a drastic reduction in power, and I&#39;m sure if everyone fell it would be a pretty lousy first bout.

&nbsp;

In addition, only healing up to the max of the zone I am in, if AZ did not having healing, he could only go up to 11 health after that encounter, essentially guarenteeing that later encounters would drop him in short order.

&nbsp;

Now, perhaps it was because of a demo game and a lot of novices at the table not used to the system, but the &quot;Minor Twists&quot; seemed fairly major when we encoutered them. We suffered extra attacks or had our abilities nullified for the entire scene, and it seemed to be far more in the DM&#39;s hands than the rules indicated, though that was probably because of the demos. I can&#39;t imagine taking more than 2 or 3 twists in a scene before my ability to contribute would be sorely limited, and starting a scene with a twist just makes the chance of the overcomes leading to lots of problems even more pronounced. And taking a major? I can barely imagine it, considering how the minors alone tended to effect us.

&nbsp;

Again, this might be a system shock for me, more time with the system will probably smooth it all out, but it was highly noticeable to me that this downward spiral of the characters seems incredibly hard to mitigate, and against multiple groups of foes, it seems like it could quickly lead to characters who are severly beaten down, and I never even saw what a lieutenant could do because we took it down so quickly, and I know the enemies can get tougher than that.

&nbsp;

It&#39;s concerning to consider for a few reasons while I&#39;m thinking on my group. They tend to either charge in with no thought, or be incredibly, frustatingly cautious. Both have problems with this system. I suspect I would see a lot of people taking armored abilities and defensive reactions, probably self-healing, making the characters I&#39;d see at the table be a little too homogenous for a superhero story, and they would slow things to a crawl when they starting actually dealing with a scene, because once you are in a bad spot, it is incredibly difficult to get back out of it.

&nbsp;

I hope I&#39;m phrasing things in a way that make my concerns clear, I&#39;m not good at explaining my reactions sometimes.
